# What the container build does not need to carry.
#
# `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile copies this folder, so anything large, private or
# irrelevant should be named here. Two of these matter beyond speed: the family's
# own backup (`backups/`) and the local Convex state (`.convex/`) hold private
# data, and neither belongs in an image.
#
# `convex_local_storage/` is the same kind of thing — the local backend's own
# files, modules and exports, eleven megabytes of this machine's storage with no
# database in the repository to read it back. Named for the same reason as the
# two above it.
node_modules
dist
.vly-run
.convex
convex_local_storage
backups
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Generated downloads of the source tree — what *saving* this project produces,
# not what it is made of. Left in place they are packed into the build context
# and then into the next archive, which is how a 21 MB context becomes 42.
# `**/` is spelled out because Docker treats a bare `*` as not crossing a
# directory separator, unlike git — where the same two lines mean the same thing
# anyway, and where the exception below is already written for the same reason.
**/*.zip
**/*.tar.gz

# ...except the two archives the app actually serves: every download on the
# Offline Brain page carries them and the installer unpacks both, so a built hub
# without them would offer buttons that produce nothing — and `bun run
# test:this-app` reads them straight out of `public/`, so a context that left
# them out could not even be checked. Both have to be named by hand:
# `**/*.zip` above excludes every zip, and a negation is the only way back in.
# (`.gitignore` keeps the same two exceptions, for the same reason.)
!public/offline-brain/source/freebuff-source.zip
!public/offline-brain/source/this-app-source.zip

# The vendored reference tree the offline brain documents (~81 MB, including
# ripgrep binaries): a reference, never compiled. `public/offline-brain/` — the
# part that ships — is kept.
offline-ai-coding-brain

# These three are read at *build* time, so they stay: `src/lib/sugar-native.ts`
# packs the whole native host into the Web Portal's download with Vite's
# `import.meta.glob`, and the same source is what the AI Builder reads. They are
# under a megabyte between them, which is cheaper than a button that does nothing.
#   native/  os/  sugar-browser/
